WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.635
I'm Dalibor Šandor.
I come from Serbia, Novi Sad.

00:00:09.635 --> 00:00:13.920
I come from the inclusive theatrical

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.683
artistic group Per.Art.

00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:21.048
I describe my work as

00:00:21.048 --> 00:00:25.393
something of a contemporary dancer

00:00:25.393 --> 00:00:28.785
but more as a performer.

00:00:29.443 --> 00:00:32.713
My name is Xavier Le Roy

00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:36.758
and I am an artist
working in the field of choreography

00:00:36.758 --> 00:00:41.070
and also, since last year,

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:47.345
I'm a Professor at the Institute
for Applied Theatre Studies in Giessen.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.645
"If you could make a performance,
what should it be called?"

00:00:53.645 --> 00:00:56.628
and I said "We Are Not Monsters".

00:00:56.628 --> 00:01:03.553
They asked me why and I said it's
because people look at us as some kind of monsters.

00:01:03.553 --> 00:01:07.718
In that way, they don't socialize with us,
they are scared of us,

00:01:07.718 --> 00:01:14.425
sometimes, they are even disgusted by us,

00:01:14.425 --> 00:01:18.948
they don't want to approach us,

00:01:18.948 --> 00:01:22.553
they want to remain in their groups,

00:01:22.553 --> 00:01:25.170
and the second factor

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:30.235
is that I really, but really, like horror.

00:01:30.235 --> 00:01:33.803
Horror literature, horror movies,

00:01:33.803 --> 00:01:36.838
horror videogames and things like that.

00:01:38.088 --> 00:01:40.835
I liked his proposal.

00:01:40.835 --> 00:01:44.828
I'm also interested in this idea of monsters.

00:01:44.828 --> 00:01:49.273
I have this sentence about monsters
from Michel Foucault always in my mind,

00:01:49.273 --> 00:01:52.470
where he says:
"We are all monsters".

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:56.355
We will see what Dalibor thinks about this.

00:01:56.355 --> 00:01:59.840
I know that, in his fantasy,

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.065
he is very connected with monsters

00:02:04.065 --> 00:02:09.433
in fantastic movies, so it's something
that he is attracted by.

00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:12.908
My relationship with monsters

00:02:12.908 --> 00:02:17.043
would be more to look to
what is monstrous in each of us

00:02:17.043 --> 00:02:20.288
or can we understand something

00:02:20.288 --> 00:02:24.350
or can we encounter
voluntarily or involuntarily,

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:29.115
consciously or unconsciously, something
that would be understood as monstrous

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:32.043
in each and every one?

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.312
Monsters are interesting to me
because they are different from the others.

00:02:39.312 --> 00:02:46.552
Yes. And this is something
you also experience and you want to share.

00:02:46.552 --> 00:02:52.713
That's also something I understood from
our week of work.

00:02:52.713 --> 00:02:57.588
To share how one can be

00:02:57.588 --> 00:03:02.398
seen by the other as different

00:03:02.398 --> 00:03:06.325
and that the difference one has

00:03:06.325 --> 00:03:13.343
is not necessarily the one
that one want to have as a difference.

00:03:13.343 --> 00:03:19.275
We have talked a lot about it this week.
This notion of yes, we are all different

00:03:19.275 --> 00:03:24.172
but the problem or the question we have

00:03:24.172 --> 00:03:28.745
is when this difference
is making the other a monster.

00:03:32.192 --> 00:03:38.272
I think the question is:
Will this difference be acceptable to society?

00:03:38.272 --> 00:03:40.405
- Yes.
- Yes.

